Subject: [PATCH] JNI: a transform binding taking several inputs, a chosen output type and nulls

Against rapidsai/cudf ff5b362d7c06ae5837fd7a7337e2ae20895f324d, which is the submodule pin of
NVIDIA/cudf-spark-jni 761c184ff0a0dc5b678b1f5f163c579068beb133 -- the revisions the released
com.nvidia:spark-rapids-jni:26.08.0:cuda12 names in its own version-info properties.

WHAT IS MISSING UPSTREAM

  libcudf's `cudf::transform_extended` already takes a span of input columns, an output
  `data_type`, a `null_aware` flag and an `output_nullability` policy. The Java side reaches none
  of that. `ColumnView.transform(String, boolean)` passes exactly one column, hard-codes
  `data_type(type_id::INT32)`, and has no parameter for null-awareness:

    JNIEXPORT jlong JNICALL Java_ai_rapids_cudf_ColumnView_transform(...)
    {
      cudf::transform_input inputs[] = {*column};
      return release_as_jlong(cudf::transform_extended(
        inputs, n_udf, cudf::data_type(cudf::type_id::INT32),
        j_is_ptx ? cudf::udf_source_type::PTX : cudf::udf_source_type::CUDA));
    }

  So the restriction is in the binding, not in the library. This patch widens the binding and
  changes nothing else: no new C++ algorithm, no change to the existing overload, and no change
  to how errors are reported.

WHAT IS ADDED

  * ai.rapids.cudf.OutputNullability -- an enum mirroring cudf::output_nullability.
  * ColumnView.transform(ColumnView[], String, boolean, DType, boolean, OutputNullability)
  * Java_ai_rapids_cudf_ColumnView_transformMulti, which forwards to transform_extended.

  The existing instance method and its JNI entry point are untouched, so every caller that
  compiles today still compiles and still runs the same code.

THE DEVICE FUNCTION'S SHAPE

  Fixed by libcudf, not by this patch. cpp/src/transform/jit/kernel.cu calls
  GENERIC_TRANSFORM_OP(out_ptrs..., ins...), so the output pointer comes first and the inputs
  follow in column order. With null_aware::NO that is

    __device__ void f(OUT *out, IN0 in0, IN1 in1, ...)

  and with null_aware::YES the kernel reads each input with `nullable_element` and gives the
  function an optional to fill, so it is

    __device__ void f(cuda::std::optional<OUT> *out, cuda::std::optional<IN0> in0, ...)

  A function may instead return a value, which the kernel casts to `cudf::errc`; that works
  through the new entry point exactly as it does through the old one, because the decision is
  made in the kernel at compile time and the binding has no part in it.

ON output_nullability

  Both values are exposed because only one of them is always correct. PRESERVE is: for a
  null-unaware function it gives the output the intersection of the input masks and skips the
  rows under a null, and for a null-aware one it takes the output's validity from what the
  function returned. ALL_VALID asserts that every row produces a value and allocates no mask;
  with a null-unaware function it also stops rows being skipped, so the function reads the raw
  value under a null. It is an optimisation with a precondition, and the precondition is not
  one the binding can check, so it is the caller's to state.

TESTS

  java/src/test/java/ai/rapids/cudf/TransformMultiTest.java covers one, two and three inputs;
  INT32, INT64, FLOAT32, FLOAT64 and BOOL8 outputs; mixed input types; zero, one and a million
  rows; all-null, mixed-null and no-null inputs; a function that answers for a null, one that
  returns a null for a non-null input, and one that unboxes a null; error codes through both the
  new and the old entry point; device memory accounted before and after both successful and
  failing calls; and the argument validation above. It runs as part of the JNI build's own Java
  test suite, on a GPU.

diff --git a/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/ColumnView.java b/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/ColumnView.java
index 5985d6ba2f..6a4258540e 100644
--- a/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/ColumnView.java
+++ b/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/ColumnView.java
@@ -1118,6 +1118,65 @@ public class ColumnView implements AutoCloseable, BinaryOperable {
     return new ColumnVector(transform(getNativeView(), udf, isPtx));
   }
 
+  /**
+   * Transform one or more columns together with a single device function.
+   * <p>
+   * This is the general form of {@link #transform(String, boolean)}: it takes any number of input
+   * columns, produces a column of a chosen type, and can run the function over nullable inputs.
+   * <p>
+   * The function's parameter list is fixed by libcudf, in this order: a pointer to the output,
+   * then one parameter per input column, in the order the columns are given here. For CUDA source
+   * with {@code isNullAware} false that is
+   * <pre>{@code __device__ void f(OUT* out, IN0 in0, IN1 in1, ...)}</pre>
+   * and with {@code isNullAware} true every value is wrapped:
+   * <pre>{@code __device__ void f(cuda::std::optional<OUT>* out,
+   *                    cuda::std::optional<IN0> in0, ...)}</pre>
+   * A function may instead return a value, which libcudf reads as a {@code cudf::errc} and
+   * reports by throwing; that works here exactly as it does for the single-input form.
+   * <p>
+   * Only the function's own name is taken from the source: libcudf renames the identifier in front
+   * of the first {@code (} it finds, so the entry point must come first in the source text.
+   *
+   * @param inputs             the input columns, all of the same row count. Not empty. The caller
+   *                           keeps them alive for the duration of the call.
+   * @param udf                the device function, as CUDA source or PTX
+   * @param isPtx              true if {@code udf} is PTX, false if it is CUDA source. PTX cannot
+   *                           be null-aware, and is limited to integral and floating-point types.
+   * @param outputType         the type of the returned column
+   * @param isNullAware        true to pass each input as an optional and take the output as an
+   *                           optional, so the function decides what a null means
+   * @param outputNullability  whether the output may carry a null mask. {@link
+   *                           OutputNullability#PRESERVE} is always safe; see that enum for when
+   *                           {@link OutputNullability#ALL_VALID} is not.
+   * @return the transformed column
+   */
+  public static ColumnVector transform(ColumnView[] inputs, String udf, boolean isPtx,
+                                       DType outputType, boolean isNullAware,
+                                       OutputNullability outputNullability) {
+    if (inputs == null || inputs.length == 0) {
+      throw new IllegalArgumentException("transform requires at least one input column");
+    }
+    if (udf == null) {
+      throw new IllegalArgumentException("udf is null");
+    }
+    if (outputType == null) {
+      throw new IllegalArgumentException("outputType is null");
+    }
+    if (outputNullability == null) {
+      throw new IllegalArgumentException("outputNullability is null");
+    }
+    long[] handles = new long[inputs.length];
+    for (int i = 0; i < inputs.length; i++) {
+      if (inputs[i] == null) {
+        throw new IllegalArgumentException("input column " + i + " is null");
+      }
+      handles[i] = inputs[i].getNativeView();
+    }
+    return new ColumnVector(transformMulti(handles, udf, isPtx,
+        outputType.getTypeId().getNativeId(), outputType.getScale(),
+        isNullAware, outputNullability.getNativeId()));
+  }
+
   /**
    * Multiple different unary operations. The output is the same type as input.
    * @param op      the operation to perform
@@ -5164,6 +5223,10 @@ public class ColumnView implements AutoCloseable, BinaryOperable {
 
   private static native long transform(long viewHandle, String udf, boolean isPtx);
 
+  private static native long transformMulti(long[] viewHandles, String udf, boolean isPtx,
+                                            int outTypeId, int outScale, boolean isNullAware,
+                                            int outputNullability);
+
   private static native long clamper(long nativeView, long loScalarHandle, long loScalarReplaceHandle,
                                      long hiScalarHandle, long hiScalarReplaceHandle);
 
diff --git a/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/OutputNullability.java b/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/OutputNullability.java
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..d724a59b09
--- /dev/null
+++ b/java/src/main/java/ai/rapids/cudf/OutputNullability.java
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+/*
+ * SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2026, NVIDIA CORPORATION.
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+ */
+
+package ai.rapids.cudf;
+
+/**
+ * Whether a transform's output column is allowed to carry a null mask.
+ * <p>
+ * This mirrors {@code cudf::output_nullability}. Which of the two is correct depends on the
+ * device function, not only on the inputs:
+ * <ul>
+ *   <li>{@link #PRESERVE} is always safe. For a null-unaware function it gives the output the
+ *       intersection of the input validity masks, and rows with a null input are not evaluated.
+ *       For a null-aware function it gives the output the validity the function itself returns.</li>
+ *   <li>{@link #ALL_VALID} asserts that the function produces a value for every row. libcudf then
+ *       allocates no mask. For a null-unaware function this also means no rows are skipped, so the
+ *       function reads the raw value under a null, which is only safe when no input is nullable.
+ *       Using it with a function that can produce a null is undefined behaviour.</li>
+ * </ul>
+ */
+public enum OutputNullability {
+  /** A null mask is produced if the operation can produce nulls. */
+  PRESERVE(0),
+  /** No null mask is produced; every output value is treated as valid. */
+  ALL_VALID(1);
+
+  private final int nativeId;
+
+  OutputNullability(int nativeId) {
+    this.nativeId = nativeId;
+  }
+
+  int getNativeId() {
+    return nativeId;
+  }
+}
diff --git a/java/src/main/native/src/ColumnViewJni.cpp b/java/src/main/native/src/ColumnViewJni.cpp
index 67b890ce5c..b0d23ee959 100644
--- a/java/src/main/native/src/ColumnViewJni.cpp
+++ b/java/src/main/native/src/ColumnViewJni.cpp
@@ -1565,6 +1565,59 @@ JNIEXPORT jlong JNICALL Java_ai_rapids_cudf_ColumnView_transform(
   JNI_CATCH(env, 0);
 }
 
+JNIEXPORT jlong JNICALL Java_ai_rapids_cudf_ColumnView_transformMulti(JNIEnv* env,
+                                                                      jclass,
+                                                                      jlongArray j_view_handles,
+                                                                      jstring j_udf,
+                                                                      jboolean j_is_ptx,
+                                                                      jint j_out_type_id,
+                                                                      jint j_out_scale,
+                                                                      jboolean j_is_null_aware,
+                                                                      jint j_output_nullability)
+{
+  JNI_NULL_CHECK(env, j_view_handles, "input column handles are null", 0);
+  JNI_NULL_CHECK(env, j_udf, "udf is null", 0);
+  JNI_ARG_CHECK(env,
+                j_output_nullability == 0 || j_output_nullability == 1,
+                "output nullability is not a cudf::output_nullability value",
+                0);
+  JNI_TRY
+  {
+    cudf::jni::auto_set_device(env);
+
+    // native_jpointerArray releases the Java array in its destructor, including on the throw
+    // paths below, so the handles are not pinned across the transform itself.
+    cudf::jni::native_jpointerArray<cudf::column_view> n_views(env, j_view_handles);
+    JNI_ARG_CHECK(env, n_views.size() > 0, "transform requires at least one input column", 0);
+
+    std::vector<cudf::transform_input> inputs;
+    inputs.reserve(n_views.size());
+    for (int i = 0; i < n_views.size(); i++) {
+      JNI_NULL_CHECK(env, n_views[i], "input column is null", 0);
+      // A transform_input holds a column_view by value, so the view is copied here and the
+      // caller's ColumnView only has to outlive this loop, not the kernel launch. The device
+      // buffers it points at are owned by the caller either way.
+      inputs.emplace_back(*n_views[i]);
+    }
+
+    cudf::jni::native_jstring n_j_udf(env, j_udf);
+    std::string n_udf = n_j_udf;
+
+    // transform_extended validates the rest: equal row counts, supported input and output types,
+    // and that PTX is not asked to be null-aware. Letting it do so keeps one set of rules.
+    return release_as_jlong(cudf::transform_extended(
+      inputs,
+      n_udf,
+      cudf::jni::make_data_type(j_out_type_id, j_out_scale),
+      j_is_ptx ? cudf::udf_source_type::PTX : cudf::udf_source_type::CUDA,
+      std::nullopt,
+      j_is_null_aware ? cudf::null_aware::YES : cudf::null_aware::NO,
+      std::nullopt,
+      static_cast<cudf::output_nullability>(j_output_nullability)));
+  }
+  JNI_CATCH(env, 0);
+}
+
 JNIEXPORT jlong JNICALL Java_ai_rapids_cudf_ColumnView_stringStartWith(JNIEnv* env,
                                                                        jobject j_object,
                                                                        jlong j_view_handle,
diff --git a/java/src/test/java/ai/rapids/cudf/TransformMultiTest.java b/java/src/test/java/ai/rapids/cudf/TransformMultiTest.java
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..fa036b67bf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/java/src/test/java/ai/rapids/cudf/TransformMultiTest.java
@@ -0,0 +1,602 @@
+/*
+ *
+ *  SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) 2026, NVIDIA CORPORATION & AFFILIATES. All rights reserved.
+ *  SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
+ *
+ */
+
+package ai.rapids.cudf;
+
+import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
+
+import java.util.Random;
+
+import static ai.rapids.cudf.AssertUtils.assertColumnsAreEqual;
+import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;
+import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;
+import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;
+
+/**
+ * Tests for the generalized {@link ColumnView#transform(ColumnView[], String, boolean, DType,
+ * boolean, OutputNullability)} binding: several inputs, a chosen output type, and null-aware
+ * execution.
+ *
+ * <p>Every expected value here is computed on the host, in Java, from the same inputs. The
+ * floating-point cases deliberately use values and operations that are exact in binary
+ * floating point -- sums and doublings of small integers -- so that the comparison does not
+ * depend on whether the JIT contracts a multiply-add or on any other reassociation the device
+ * compiler is free to make. Where a test does care about an inexact result it says so.
+ */
+public class TransformMultiTest extends CudfTestBase {
+
+  private static ColumnVector transform(String udf, DType outType, ColumnView... inputs) {
+    return ColumnView.transform(inputs, udf, false, outType, false, OutputNullability.PRESERVE);
+  }
+
+  private static ColumnVector transformNullAware(String udf, DType outType,
+                                                 OutputNullability nullability,
+                                                 ColumnView... inputs) {
+    return ColumnView.transform(inputs, udf, false, outType, true, nullability);
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // A: INT32 -> INT32. The same function the single-input binding is tested with, so this
+  // also says the generalized path agrees with the one that already shipped.
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  private static final String A_UDF =
+      "__device__ inline void f(int* out, int a) { *out = a * a - a; }";
+
+  @Test
+  void caseA_int32ToInt32() {
+    // Deliberately small values: signed overflow is undefined in C++, so a test that relied on
+    // it wrapping would be testing nvcc, not the binding. Boundary values get their own test
+    // below, with a function that cannot overflow.
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(2, 3, null, 4, -5, 0, 1000, -1000);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(A_UDF, DType.INT32, in);
+         ColumnVector viaOld = in.transform(A_UDF, false);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(
+             2 * 2 - 2, 3 * 3 - 3, null, 4 * 4 - 4, -5 * -5 - -5, 0,
+             1000 * 1000 - 1000, -1000 * -1000 - -1000)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      // The old binding must keep working, and must agree with the new one.
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(viaOld, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void integerBoundaryValues() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(long long* out, int a, long long b) "
+        + "{ *out = (long long)a + b / 2; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromInts(Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE, 0, -1);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromLongs(Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE, 0L, -1L);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.INT64, a, b);
+         // Integer division truncates towards zero in both Java and C++, so -1 / 2 == 0.
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromLongs(
+             (long) Integer.MIN_VALUE + Long.MIN_VALUE / 2,
+             (long) Integer.MAX_VALUE + Long.MAX_VALUE / 2,
+             0L,
+             -1L)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // B: INT32, INT32 -> INT32
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  @Test
+  void caseB_twoInt32ToInt32() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(int* out, int a, int b) { *out = a + b * 2; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromInts(1, 2, 3, -4, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromInts(10, -20, 30, 40, 0, 0);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.INT32, a, b);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromInts(
+             1 + 10 * 2, 2 + -20 * 2, 3 + 30 * 2, -4 + 40 * 2, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseB_argumentOrderIsTheColumnOrder() {
+    // A non-commutative function, so that swapping the columns must change the answer.
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(int* out, int a, int b) { *out = a - b; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromInts(10, 20, 30);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromInts(1, 2, 3);
+         ColumnVector ab = transform(udf, DType.INT32, a, b);
+         ColumnVector ba = transform(udf, DType.INT32, b, a);
+         ColumnVector expectedAb = ColumnVector.fromInts(9, 18, 27);
+         ColumnVector expectedBa = ColumnVector.fromInts(-9, -18, -27)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expectedAb, ab);
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expectedBa, ba);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // C: INT32, FLOAT32 -> FLOAT64
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  @Test
+  void caseC_mixedInt32Float32ToFloat64() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(double* out, int a, float b) "
+        + "{ *out = (double)a + (double)b; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromInts(1, 2, 3, -4, 0);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromFloats(0.5f, 0.25f, -1.5f, 2.0f, 0.0f);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.FLOAT64, a, b);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromDoubles(1.5, 2.25, 1.5, -2.0, 0.0)) {
+      // Every value above is a dyadic rational exactly representable in both float and double,
+      // and a single addition of two exact doubles is itself exact, so this is an equality test.
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseC_floatingPointSpecialValues() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(double* out, int a, float b) "
+        + "{ *out = (double)a + (double)b; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromInts(1, 1, 1, 1);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromFloats(Float.NaN, Float.POSITIVE_INFINITY,
+             Float.NEGATIVE_INFINITY, -0.0f);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.FLOAT64, a, b);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromDoubles(Double.NaN, Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY,
+             Double.NEGATIVE_INFINITY, 1.0)) {
+      // assertColumnsAreEqual compares NaN bit patterns through the host column, and
+      // float -> double widening of a quiet NaN keeps it quiet.
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // D: INT64, INT32, FLOAT64 -> INT64
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  @Test
+  void caseD_threeMixedInputsToInt64() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(long long* out, long long a, int b, double c) "
+        + "{ *out = a + (long long)b + (long long)c; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromLongs(1L, 1L << 40, -7L, 0L);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromInts(2, 3, -4, 0);
+         ColumnVector c = ColumnVector.fromDoubles(3.0, 4.75, -5.25, 0.0);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.INT64, a, b, c);
+         // C and C++ truncate towards zero when converting double to an integer, which is what
+         // (long long)c does: 4.75 -> 4, -5.25 -> -5.
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromLongs(
+             1L + 2L + 3L, (1L << 40) + 3L + 4L, -7L + -4L + -5L, 0L)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // E: INT64 -> INT64, and F: FLOAT64 -> FLOAT64
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  @Test
+  void caseE_int64ToInt64() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(long long* out, long long a) { *out = a * 3; }";
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(1L, -2L, null, 1L << 40, 0L,
+             Long.MIN_VALUE / 4, Long.MAX_VALUE / 4);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.INT64, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(3L, -6L, null, 3L * (1L << 40), 0L,
+             (Long.MIN_VALUE / 4) * 3, (Long.MAX_VALUE / 4) * 3)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseF_float64ToFloat64() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(double* out, double a) { *out = a + a; }";
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedDoubles(1.5, -2.25, null, 0.0, -0.0,
+             Double.MAX_VALUE / 4);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.FLOAT64, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedDoubles(3.0, -4.5, null, 0.0, -0.0,
+             Double.MAX_VALUE / 2)) {
+      // Doubling is exact in binary floating point for every finite input that does not overflow.
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void boolOutput() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(bool* out, long long a) { *out = a > 0; }";
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(1L, -2L, null, 0L, 7L);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.BOOL8, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedBooleans(true, false, null, false, true)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void float32Output() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(float* out, int a) { *out = (float)a * 0.5f; }";
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromInts(1, 2, 3, -4);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.FLOAT32, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromFloats(0.5f, 1.0f, 1.5f, -2.0f)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // Null handling without null-awareness: libcudf builds a stencil from the inputs, skips
+  // those rows, and gives the output the intersection of the input masks.
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  @Test
+  void nullUnawareIntersectsInputValidity() {
+    String udf = "__device__ inline void f(int* out, int a, int b) { *out = a + b; }";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(1, null, 3, null, 5);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(10, 20, null, null, 50);
+         ColumnVector got = transform(udf, DType.INT32, a, b);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(11, null, null, null, 55)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // G: nullable INT64 -> non-null INT32. The function answers for a null input, so the
+  // output carries no mask at all.
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  private static final String G_UDF =
+      "__device__ inline void f(cuda::std::optional<int>* out, cuda::std::optional<long long> a) "
+          + "{ *out = a.has_value() ? (int)a.value() : -1; }";
+
+  @Test
+  void caseG_nullableInt64ToNonNullInt32() {
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(5L, null, -7L, null, 0L);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(G_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.ALL_VALID, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromInts(5, -1, -7, -1, 0)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      assertEquals(0, got.getNullCount());
+      assertTrue(!got.hasNulls());
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseG_alsoCorrectUnderPreserve() {
+    // PRESERVE is always safe: the mask is then whatever the function returned, which here is
+    // "valid" for every row. The values must be identical to the ALL_VALID run.
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(5L, null, -7L, null, 0L);
+         ColumnVector allValid = transformNullAware(G_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.ALL_VALID, in);
+         ColumnVector preserve = transformNullAware(G_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.PRESERVE, in)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(allValid, preserve);
+      assertEquals(0, preserve.getNullCount());
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseG_allNullInput() {
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(null, null, null);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(G_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.ALL_VALID, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromInts(-1, -1, -1)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+    }
+  }
+
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+  // H: nullable INT64 -> nullable INT32. The function returns null for a null input and also
+  // for a non-null input it does not like, which is the case a validity-mask copy cannot do.
+  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+  private static final String H_UDF =
+      "__device__ inline void f(cuda::std::optional<int>* out, cuda::std::optional<long long> a) {"
+          + "  if (!a.has_value()) { *out = cuda::std::nullopt; return; }"
+          + "  if (a.value() < 0) { *out = cuda::std::nullopt; return; }"
+          + "  *out = (int)(a.value() * 2);"
+          + "}";
+
+  @Test
+  void caseH_nullableInt64ToNullableInt32() {
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(5L, null, -7L, 0L, 11L);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(H_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.PRESERVE, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(10, null, null, 0, 22)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      // Row 2 was not null on input but is null on output: the mask is the function's, not a
+      // copy of the input's.
+      assertEquals(2, got.getNullCount());
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseH_nullProducedFromAFullyValidInput() {
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromLongs(1L, -1L, 2L, -2L);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(H_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.PRESERVE, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(2, null, 4, null)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      assertEquals(2, got.getNullCount());
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void caseH_allNullInput() {
+    try (ColumnVector in = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(null, null, null, null);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(H_UDF, DType.INT32,
+             OutputNullability.PRESERVE, in);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(null, null, null, null)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      assertEquals(4, got.getNullCount());
+    }
+  }
+
+  @Test
+  void nullAwareWithTwoInputsEitherOfWhichMayBeNull() {
+    String udf =
+        "__device__ inline void f(cuda::std::optional<long long>* out,"
+            + " cuda::std::optional<long long> a, cuda::std::optional<int> b) {"
+            + "  if (!a.has_value() && !b.has_value()) { *out = cuda::std::nullopt; return; }"
+            + "  long long av = a.has_value() ? a.value() : 0;"
+            + "  long long bv = b.has_value() ? (long long)b.value() : 0;"
+            + "  *out = av + bv;"
+            + "}";
+    try (ColumnVector a = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(1L, null, 3L, null, 5L);
+         ColumnVector b = ColumnVector.fromBoxedInts(10, 20, null, null, 50);
+         ColumnVector got = transformNullAware(udf, DType.INT64,
+             OutputNullability.PRESERVE, a, b);
+         ColumnVector expected = ColumnVector.fromBoxedLongs(11L, 20L, 3L, null, 55L)) {
+      assertColumnsAreEqual(expected, got);
+      assertEquals(1, got.getNullCount());
+    }
+  }
